1
00:01:14,308 --> 00:01:18,336
A shelter that is so desperately
needed by the city's homeless families,

2
00:01:18,512 --> 00:01:22,347
particularly its children,
is finally a reality.

3
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:26,746
And so I gratefully accept
this award that you've given me.

4
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:31,016
But really, this award belongs
to you and to the community,

5
00:01:31,191 --> 00:01:32,551
for all the work
that you've done.

6
00:01:32,693 --> 00:01:34,787
And so I really... I
just want to thank you,

7
00:01:34,962 --> 00:01:36,002
each and every one of you,

8
00:01:36,163 --> 00:01:38,689
for all your support and
generosity. Thank you very much.

9
00:01:51,044 --> 00:01:53,240
It's so good to see you.

10
00:01:53,413 --> 00:01:54,972
Hi.

11
00:01:55,515 --> 00:01:57,245
Steven?

12
00:01:57,784 --> 00:02:00,982
Steven, would you be
an angel, right over here.

13
00:02:01,154 --> 00:02:03,953
Leanne? Leanne
McIntyre, is that you?

14
00:02:04,124 --> 00:02:05,244
Hello, Phyllis. How are you?

15
00:02:05,859 --> 00:02:09,261
Oh, busy as ever.
Just go, go, go.

16
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:11,496
Well, let's take a look at you.

17
00:02:13,867 --> 00:02:15,267
I didn't know you knew Gil.

18
00:02:15,602 --> 00:02:16,865
Well, actually I don't,

19
00:02:17,037 --> 00:02:19,404
but I agreed to do some
legal aid work for the shelter.

20
00:02:19,573 --> 00:02:21,132
Isn't that sweet?

21
00:02:21,742 --> 00:02:23,335
Oh, speak of the devil.

22
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:26,207
Phyllis, I've gotta meet
Gordon at the office.

23
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:29,282
Gil Stoddard, Leanne McIntyre.

24
00:02:29,449 --> 00:02:30,473
Hi, how do you do?

25
00:02:30,651 --> 00:02:32,950
- Nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet... You too.

26
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:34,452
- Congratulations.
- Thank you.

27
00:02:34,621 --> 00:02:36,141
Thank you very much
for coming. Thanks.

28
00:02:36,289 --> 00:02:37,689
- Bye, Phyllis.
- Bye.

29
00:02:55,676 --> 00:02:57,542
I'm Gil Stoddard.
These are my offices.

30
00:02:58,979 --> 00:03:01,710
Hey, give me a hand
here in the hallway.

31
00:03:02,082 --> 00:03:03,106
Gordon!

32
00:03:03,283 --> 00:03:04,627
- You can't come in.
- Gordon!

33
00:03:04,651 --> 00:03:06,691
Hold it right there!
- Let him go!

34
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:08,140
- Where's Gordon?
- Let him go!

35
00:03:09,923 --> 00:03:11,050
Where's Gordon?

36
00:03:11,224 --> 00:03:12,783
- Mr. Stoddard?
- Yes?

37
00:03:12,959 --> 00:03:14,759
I'm Lieutenant Harmon
Andrews, Atlanta Police.

38
00:03:14,895 --> 00:03:17,135
What's happened here? Damn
it, where's the hell's Gordon?

39
00:03:17,297 --> 00:03:20,131
I'm sorry, Mr. Stoddard.
There's no easy way to say this.

40
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:21,393
Your partner's dead.

41
00:03:22,769 --> 00:03:24,409
Well, no. No, no, no.
That's not possible.

42
00:03:24,538 --> 00:03:26,166
He was murdered
earlier this evening.

43
00:03:26,339 --> 00:03:27,967
Oh, my God.

44
00:03:28,141 --> 00:03:30,167
Oh, God, no, no, no!

45
00:03:31,344 --> 00:03:33,006
How? When?

46
00:03:33,447 --> 00:03:36,212
We assume the murderer gained
access through the back door.

47
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:39,781
- But we have an alarm.
- It wasn't on.

48
00:03:39,953 --> 00:03:42,752
My guess is it was
early. Mr. Lyte felt safe.

49
00:03:42,923 --> 00:03:46,291
He heard something, though, because
he pressed the alarm in his office.

50
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:49,521
The alarm company
received the call at 9:27.

51
00:03:50,097 --> 00:03:51,857
When my man got here
about six minutes later,

52
00:03:51,998 --> 00:03:54,194
- they found him dead.
- He didn't go to the banquet

53
00:03:54,367 --> 00:03:58,031
because we had this
big presentation to make.

54
00:03:59,005 --> 00:04:00,906
He'd still be alive if
he'd gone to the banquet.

55
00:04:01,074 --> 00:04:03,154
Mr. Stoddard, there's no
need for you to go up there.

56
00:04:03,310 --> 00:04:04,334
Mr. Stoddard!

57
00:04:10,951 --> 00:04:13,887
Oh, God. ANDREWS: Easy, easy.

58
00:04:14,287 --> 00:04:15,482
Jimmy, wet me a cloth.

59
00:04:15,655 --> 00:04:18,056
Lieutenant Andrews, we're
ready to spray the room.

60
00:04:18,325 --> 00:04:20,021
Yeah, okay, okay. Go ahead.

61
00:04:22,195 --> 00:04:23,959
Here, put it on your neck.

62
00:04:26,199 --> 00:04:28,134
I think you ought
to go downstairs.

63
00:04:28,301 --> 00:04:30,031
No, it's...

64
00:04:30,337 --> 00:04:31,862
I'm okay. I am.

65
00:04:32,038 --> 00:04:33,558
It's not necessary
for you to be here.

66
00:04:33,707 --> 00:04:35,733
Damn it, I need to know
what happened here.

67
00:04:37,844 --> 00:04:39,403
Okay.

68
00:04:41,181 --> 00:04:43,514
Apparently, there
was quite a struggle.

69
00:04:44,651 --> 00:04:46,552
He was stabbed a number of times

70
00:04:47,988 --> 00:04:50,082
- and then strangled.
- No.

71
00:04:50,257 --> 00:04:52,749
- Strangled?
- With a wire.

72
00:04:52,926 --> 00:04:56,328
The lab boys here are
spraying with Lumaglow.

73
00:04:56,496 --> 00:04:58,590
It's a chemical that
picks up blood stains,

74
00:04:59,065 --> 00:05:01,296
including any the killer
might have tried to wash away.

75
00:05:01,468 --> 00:05:03,664
It would cause them
to glow in the dark.

76
00:05:03,837 --> 00:05:08,275
Now, by doing this, we can pinpoint
exactly where the crime took place,

77
00:05:08,441 --> 00:05:11,001
and hopefully we can
reconstruct how it happened.

78
00:05:11,178 --> 00:05:13,943
You're right. I don't
need to see this.

79
00:05:17,217 --> 00:05:18,742
All right, let's do it now.

80
00:05:18,919 --> 00:05:20,945
Hey, you wanna kill the lights?

81
00:05:21,721 --> 00:05:25,920
Jeez. What kind of
animal would do this?

82
00:05:26,693 --> 00:05:28,093
Surely goodness and mercy

83
00:05:28,261 --> 00:05:30,696
shall follow me all
the days of my life,

84
00:05:30,864 --> 00:05:33,766
and I will dwell in the
house of the Lord forever.

85
00:05:33,934 --> 00:05:36,836
Amen. MOURNERS: Amen.

86
00:05:43,410 --> 00:05:48,815
Gordon was my partner,

87
00:05:48,982 --> 00:05:51,816
my mentor and my dearest friend.

88
00:05:51,985 --> 00:05:53,783
He brought joy and
wisdom to all of us

89
00:05:53,954 --> 00:05:58,050
and our lives are
less for his passing.

90
00:06:07,834 --> 00:06:10,963
I know how much you
loved your brother, Richard.

91
00:06:11,137 --> 00:06:14,232
Richard, we both loved him.

92
00:06:14,407 --> 00:06:15,431
And now we've lost him,

93
00:06:15,609 --> 00:06:19,137
and I was just hoping that
somehow, through all this grief,

94
00:06:19,312 --> 00:06:20,940
we could start over.

95
00:06:21,114 --> 00:06:22,309
Why?

96
00:06:22,482 --> 00:06:24,815
So I can sit back and shut up?

97
00:06:24,985 --> 00:06:28,183
Watch you destroy the
business that my brother built?

98
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:35,495
I don't think so.

99
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:14,863
What do you say, Harmon?

100
00:07:15,035 --> 00:07:16,799
Good to see you,
Ben. How you doing?

101
00:07:16,970 --> 00:07:22,773
Well, fresh biscuits, blueberry
pancakes, sourwood honey.

102
00:07:22,943 --> 00:07:24,571
- Can't get much better.
- Mm-hm.

103
00:07:24,744 --> 00:07:27,043
- Sit down.
- All right.

104
00:07:27,213 --> 00:07:29,739
I've been reading
about a friend of yours.

105
00:07:29,916 --> 00:07:31,748
- Oh, yeah?
- Yeah, Gil Stoddard.

106
00:07:33,019 --> 00:07:35,284
You are investigating his
partner's murder, aren't you?

107
00:07:35,455 --> 00:07:36,616
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

108
00:07:36,790 --> 00:07:37,985
You got anything?

109
00:07:38,158 --> 00:07:40,184
Well, not a heck of a lot.

110
00:07:40,360 --> 00:07:42,795
We think Gordon Lyte heard
somebody enter the building

111
00:07:42,963 --> 00:07:44,625
because the emergency
alarm was pressed.

112
00:07:46,066 --> 00:07:49,127
- You want some breakfast?
- Uh, yeah.

113
00:07:49,302 --> 00:07:52,568
- I'll have what he's having.
- Go ahead.

114
00:07:53,373 --> 00:07:55,706
- Here. This is the alarm panel.
- Mm.

115
00:07:55,875 --> 00:07:57,435
In order to set off
the emergency alarm,

116
00:07:57,610 --> 00:07:58,771
you gotta put a code in here.

117
00:07:58,945 --> 00:08:01,847
Then you gotta press these two
buttons at the same time like this.

118
00:08:02,415 --> 00:08:06,045
So you think that Gordon
Lyte probably heard something

119
00:08:06,219 --> 00:08:08,347
and then pressed
these two buttons?

120
00:08:08,521 --> 00:08:09,801
- That's what we thought.
- Yeah.

121
00:08:09,956 --> 00:08:12,084
But when the lab
boys dusted the panel,

122
00:08:12,258 --> 00:08:13,920
Gordon Lyte's
fingerprints weren't on it.

123
00:08:14,094 --> 00:08:15,118
Nobody's were.

124
00:08:15,295 --> 00:08:19,460
Oh, oh. So you think
somebody else set off the alarm

125
00:08:19,632 --> 00:08:21,533
and then wiped
these buttons clean?

126
00:08:21,701 --> 00:08:24,227
Mm-hm. And I think that
somebody else was his partner.

127
00:08:24,938 --> 00:08:25,962
You're kidding.

128
00:08:26,673 --> 00:08:30,269
Wait, the man's devoted his
life to the homeless. I mean...

129
00:08:30,643 --> 00:08:33,203
Oh, come on, Ben. I mean,
who else would have done it?

130
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:35,144
Who else would have
known about the alarm?

131
00:08:35,315 --> 00:08:36,544
The man had no enemies.

132
00:08:36,716 --> 00:08:39,652
- What's Gil Stoddard's motive?
- Greed.

133
00:08:39,819 --> 00:08:41,344
The whole company's his.

134
00:08:41,521 --> 00:08:44,286
- Well, he set himself up pretty good.
- Yeah.

135
00:08:44,457 --> 00:08:46,858
- Around 8 million good.
- Oh, heh, heh.

136
00:08:47,794 --> 00:08:50,923
Well, he has an alibi I'm sure.

137
00:08:51,097 --> 00:08:52,224
Well, yes and no.

138
00:08:52,399 --> 00:08:55,335
He was the guest of honor over at
a dinner at the Atlanta Towers Hotel.

139
00:08:55,502 --> 00:08:57,061
Now, nobody remembers
seeing him leave,

140
00:08:57,237 --> 00:08:58,914
but that doesn't mean he
couldn't have sneaked out

141
00:08:58,938 --> 00:09:01,203
and then sneaked back in
without anybody noticing.

142
00:09:01,374 --> 00:09:03,536
- Oh, yeah, there you go.
- Well, thank you.

143
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:05,474
It looks good. MATLOCK: Mm.

144
00:09:05,645 --> 00:09:08,046
I know he had a
reason to kill him.

145
00:09:08,515 --> 00:09:10,882
I know he had the opportunity.

146
00:09:11,551 --> 00:09:13,315
I just don't know
how to nail him.

147
00:09:13,486 --> 00:09:17,253
Well, maybe he'll
trip himself up. Good?

148
00:09:17,757 --> 00:09:18,986
Yeah, boy.

149
00:09:19,159 --> 00:09:20,491
- Sourwood honey.
- I'd love that.

150
00:09:20,660 --> 00:09:22,754
Mm-hm.

151
00:09:38,878 --> 00:09:42,508
Is there a Lieutenant
Andrews here?

152
00:09:43,817 --> 00:09:46,116
I'm Lieutenant
Andrews. May I help you?

153
00:09:47,087 --> 00:09:49,386
I wanna talk to you
about the Lyte murder.

154
00:09:51,424 --> 00:09:53,504
<i>In a surprise
announcement this morning,</i>

155
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:56,824
<i>the district attorney made public
the indictment of Gil Stoddard...</i>

156
00:09:56,996 --> 00:09:59,397
Okay, Dad, I'm
leaving! I'll see you later!

157
00:09:59,566 --> 00:10:01,660
- Well, I'll be...
- What did you say?

158
00:10:01,835 --> 00:10:03,667
<i>Atlanta's largest
and most successful...</i>

159
00:10:03,837 --> 00:10:06,705
Harmon Andrews just
arrested Gil Stoddard

160
00:10:06,873 --> 00:10:08,364
for the murder of his partner.

161
00:10:08,541 --> 00:10:09,941
After all this time?

162
00:10:10,110 --> 00:10:12,944
Well, Harmon told me,
what, five months ago,

163
00:10:13,113 --> 00:10:17,517
that he was trying to nab
him, but he didn't have cause.

164
00:10:17,684 --> 00:10:19,482
What changed?

165
00:10:19,686 --> 00:10:23,185
Well, um... A lady, I guess...

166
00:10:23,356 --> 00:10:28,420
A lady of the night, I
guess, named Cindy...

167
00:10:28,628 --> 00:10:30,961
I believe they said Forman.

168
00:10:31,131 --> 00:10:33,691
Came home from spending
the winter in San Diego.

169
00:10:34,267 --> 00:10:37,795
And she heard what happened
and went right down and told Harmon.

170
00:10:37,971 --> 00:10:41,806
And she said that two nights
before Gordon Lyte was killed,

171
00:10:41,975 --> 00:10:44,877
she spent the... You
know, the night with him.

172
00:10:45,044 --> 00:10:46,979
And she said he was a nice guy.

173
00:10:47,147 --> 00:10:49,639
And he said that Gil
Stoddard was a bum

174
00:10:49,816 --> 00:10:53,184
and he was going to dissolve
the partnership and get rid of him.

175
00:10:54,754 --> 00:10:55,778
What?

176
00:10:56,856 --> 00:10:58,825
I don't know. I haven't
been following the case,

177
00:10:58,992 --> 00:11:01,427
but with all the charity work
he does, you wouldn't think...

178
00:11:01,594 --> 00:11:03,034
Would you turn
that up a little bit?

179
00:11:03,396 --> 00:11:07,663
<i>Mr. Doyle, were you surprised by
these charges at all? How do you feel?</i>

180
00:11:08,168 --> 00:11:09,431
You met him, didn't you?

181
00:11:09,602 --> 00:11:10,661
Briefly.

182
00:11:10,837 --> 00:11:13,136
I'd be surprised if he
remembered my name.

183
00:11:13,306 --> 00:11:16,970
Why do you think it took so
long to press these charges?

184
00:11:17,143 --> 00:11:18,487
What about this indictment?

185
00:11:18,511 --> 00:11:19,774
No comment.

186
00:11:19,946 --> 00:11:22,707
Will you just give us...?
- Is there any way...?

187
00:11:24,250 --> 00:11:25,616
How can they do this to me?

188
00:11:25,785 --> 00:11:28,220
How could anyone
believe I would kill Gordon?

189
00:11:28,388 --> 00:11:30,482
I've gotta tell you. They've
got a very strong case.

190
00:11:30,657 --> 00:11:34,219
I didn't... I couldn't do something
like this, not to anybody.

191
00:11:34,394 --> 00:11:36,226
Look, you're in very
deep trouble, my friend,

192
00:11:36,396 --> 00:11:37,796
and you're gonna
need the very best.

193
00:11:37,964 --> 00:11:40,900
So, what do you say we
get Bailey or Dershowitz?

194
00:11:41,067 --> 00:11:43,002
Ah, I don't know.

195
00:11:43,169 --> 00:11:45,001
All right. Then how
about Ben Matlock?

196
00:11:45,405 --> 00:11:46,896
Maybe.

197
00:11:48,141 --> 00:11:50,076
How about Leanne?

198
00:11:58,952 --> 00:12:00,792
That's one of my
conditions, Mr. Doyle.

199
00:12:01,621 --> 00:12:04,682
All right, if he's willing to do
that, I'll consider representing him.

200
00:12:04,857 --> 00:12:07,759
I'll give him my other
conditions when I see him.

201
00:12:08,261 --> 00:12:11,026
You too, thanks a lot. Bye-bye.

202
00:12:11,197 --> 00:12:12,631
You going to take
the Stoddard case?

203
00:12:12,799 --> 00:12:14,131
Well, I said I'd consider it

204
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:16,860
if he consents to
taking a lie-detector test.

205
00:12:17,036 --> 00:12:19,005
It's gonna be interesting.

206
00:12:19,272 --> 00:12:21,764
I hear Harmon's got a pretty
strong case against him.

207
00:12:21,941 --> 00:12:24,308
Maybe, but the Atlanta
Police have had strong cases

208
00:12:24,477 --> 00:12:25,604
against a lot of people.

209
00:12:25,778 --> 00:12:29,078
It never stopped someone I
know from representing them.

210
00:12:29,249 --> 00:12:32,344
Nothing like being
hung by your own history.

211
00:12:32,518 --> 00:12:33,986
What if he fails the test?

212
00:12:34,153 --> 00:12:35,951
I drop out.

213
00:12:36,122 --> 00:12:37,590
I'm gonna pick him up at 5:30.

214
00:12:37,757 --> 00:12:40,124
- Wish me luck.
- Good luck.

215
00:12:46,933 --> 00:12:49,232
I think you should take a
leave of absence from work

216
00:12:49,402 --> 00:12:51,030
and keep as low a
profile as you can.

217
00:12:51,204 --> 00:12:53,696
Okay. Anything else?

218
00:12:53,873 --> 00:12:55,068
Yes.

219
00:12:55,241 --> 00:12:58,439
I drop clients the moment
I find out they've lied to me.

220
00:12:58,611 --> 00:13:00,603
Not to worry. I
don't plan on lying.

221
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:02,180
Good.

222
00:13:02,348 --> 00:13:04,579
I think I've made
the right choice.

223
00:13:05,585 --> 00:13:07,850
<i>What did you see when
you arrived at the office?</i>

224
00:13:08,388 --> 00:13:09,879
Blood.

225
00:13:13,159 --> 00:13:16,152
It was, uh, on the rug.

226
00:13:16,329 --> 00:13:18,457
It was on the cushions.

227
00:13:18,831 --> 00:13:20,595
It was everywhere.

228
00:13:22,502 --> 00:13:25,336
Gordon's secretary
packed up most of his things,

229
00:13:25,505 --> 00:13:28,031
and she had the room redone,
but it didn't make any difference.

230
00:13:28,207 --> 00:13:31,541
I haven't stepped into this
room since it happened.

231
00:13:35,515 --> 00:13:38,035
You don't look well. Why don't
we go somewhere else and do this?

232
00:13:38,184 --> 00:13:39,584
Yeah.

233
00:13:59,772 --> 00:14:02,503
This must be very
difficult for you.

234
00:14:03,009 --> 00:14:04,807
Yes, it is.

235
00:14:05,445 --> 00:14:07,073
Thank you.

236
00:14:20,126 --> 00:14:25,360
So I heard you were divorced.

237
00:14:25,531 --> 00:14:27,056
Yes.

238
00:14:28,935 --> 00:14:31,962
You, of course, know our
good friend, Phyllis Ridgeway.

239
00:14:32,138 --> 00:14:34,300
- Oh, yes.
- At the banquet for the shelter,

240
00:14:34,474 --> 00:14:37,933
she said she heard that nasty old
husband of mine had dumped me.

241
00:14:39,345 --> 00:14:41,109
I won't call her
what I'm thinking.

242
00:14:42,682 --> 00:14:46,585
She said she also thought that
throwing myself into charity work

243
00:14:46,753 --> 00:14:50,121
would just banish those
dumped-woman blues.

244
00:14:50,890 --> 00:14:52,620
If that woman didn't
know so many people,

245
00:14:52,792 --> 00:14:54,512
I would not have her
on the shelter's board.

246
00:15:00,466 --> 00:15:02,560
- Was it hard?
- What's that?

247
00:15:03,636 --> 00:15:04,968
In the divorce?

248
00:15:06,672 --> 00:15:07,731
Yeah.

249
00:15:07,907 --> 00:15:10,138
At first it was real
hard, but now it's okay.

250
00:15:10,309 --> 00:15:12,210
Yeah? Me too.

251
00:15:12,478 --> 00:15:13,639
- Oh, yeah? You're...?
- Yeah.

252
00:15:13,813 --> 00:15:15,748
Yeah, I'm a reject. Heh-heh-heh.

253
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:17,781
What is it they say...?

254
00:15:17,950 --> 00:15:20,749
Marriage is forever, love...

255
00:15:21,988 --> 00:15:24,651
Yeah, well, maybe for some.

256
00:15:24,824 --> 00:15:27,259
Yeah, maybe for some.

257
00:15:32,231 --> 00:15:36,635
Well, I have a
month to build a case.

258
00:15:36,803 --> 00:15:37,827
You ready?

259
00:15:38,004 --> 00:15:39,939
Ready as I'll ever be.

260
00:15:40,940 --> 00:15:42,841
Why'd you go back
to the office that night?

261
00:15:43,009 --> 00:15:46,036
Ah, well, as usual, Gordon
was burning the midnight oil.

262
00:15:46,212 --> 00:15:47,612
He was working on
a new ad campaign,

263
00:15:47,747 --> 00:15:50,239
and he wanted me to take a
look at it before the presentation.

264
00:15:52,018 --> 00:15:55,386
And you weren't there any
other time earlier that evening?

265
00:15:55,555 --> 00:15:58,491
Gordon was like a
brother to me. I loved him.

266
00:15:58,658 --> 00:16:00,456
I did not kill him.

267
00:16:09,502 --> 00:16:10,970
Hi.

268
00:16:11,137 --> 00:16:13,368
- Oh.
- What are you doing here?

269
00:16:13,539 --> 00:16:15,007
Uh, an appointment.

270
00:16:15,174 --> 00:16:17,109
I thought maybe we'd have lunch.

271
00:16:17,276 --> 00:16:19,905
Oh, well, this is going
to take a little while.

272
00:16:20,079 --> 00:16:22,378
Oh, Dad, I'd like you to
meet my client, Gil Stoddard.

273
00:16:22,548 --> 00:16:24,588
- Gil, my father, Ben Matlock.
- Sir, it's an honor.

274
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:26,241
Nice to meet you.

275
00:16:26,419 --> 00:16:28,650
Uh, I can hang around.

276
00:16:28,821 --> 00:16:30,687
Okay.

277
00:16:31,057 --> 00:16:33,720
Well, you better go inside.
I don't want you to be late.

278
00:16:54,113 --> 00:16:56,139
He looks calm.

279
00:16:57,850 --> 00:17:02,481
Juries like to believe that guys like
him, who have everything, are guilty.

280
00:17:02,655 --> 00:17:05,352
It makes life seem fairer.

281
00:17:05,958 --> 00:17:08,484
I never thought this
case would be easy.

282
00:17:08,661 --> 00:17:10,129
Mm-hm.

283
00:17:16,335 --> 00:17:18,895
He passed, Leanne. A-plus.

284
00:17:19,071 --> 00:17:21,404
- Straight as an arrow.
- The machine loved him.

285
00:17:21,574 --> 00:17:22,906
- That's great.
- I'll see you.

286
00:17:23,075 --> 00:17:25,237
- Thanks a lot.
- You bet.

287
00:17:28,114 --> 00:17:31,380
I wouldn't hang my hat on that.

288
00:17:31,551 --> 00:17:33,991
What do you mean, Dad? You
think the polygraph made a mistake?

289
00:17:34,120 --> 00:17:35,349
Well, no. Well...

290
00:17:35,521 --> 00:17:37,683
- You remember Gus Whitlow?
- Sure.

291
00:17:37,857 --> 00:17:40,417
I mean, he passed
two polygraphs.

292
00:17:40,593 --> 00:17:42,061
And would have gotten off

293
00:17:42,228 --> 00:17:45,460
if the police hadn't decided
to dig up his backyard anyway.

294
00:17:45,631 --> 00:17:47,691
And they found bodies
going back 14 years.

295
00:17:47,867 --> 00:17:50,462
- I remember.
- Gus was some kind of psychopath.

296
00:17:50,636 --> 00:17:54,767
Sometimes a psychopath
can fool a lie detector.

297
00:17:55,241 --> 00:17:56,641
So, what are you saying, Dad?

298
00:17:56,809 --> 00:17:58,937
You think Gil Stoddard
is a psychopath?

299
00:18:00,212 --> 00:18:02,681
Well, no. Just I...

300
00:18:03,816 --> 00:18:04,977
I don't trust him.

301
00:18:05,685 --> 00:18:11,784
- Why?
- I don't know. It's just a gut feeling.

302
00:18:12,925 --> 00:18:14,791
But he's not my client.
He's yours, Leanne.

303
00:18:14,961 --> 00:18:18,693
And if you do believe him,
well, you gotta go with it.

304
00:18:18,864 --> 00:18:22,096
I do, and I am.

305
00:18:31,043 --> 00:18:34,411
Would anybody else have
had a key to your offices?

306
00:18:34,580 --> 00:18:37,573
Uh, cleaning staff, secretaries.

307
00:18:37,750 --> 00:18:39,412
I'll speak to them.
Anybody else?

308
00:18:39,585 --> 00:18:41,110
Hmm.

309
00:18:41,821 --> 00:18:43,813
I don't think so.

310
00:18:44,957 --> 00:18:47,188
Mm. Medium or rare?

311
00:18:48,461 --> 00:18:50,020
Medium.

312
00:18:51,030 --> 00:18:53,363
How about any other
staff? Maybe a relative?

313
00:18:54,900 --> 00:18:56,630
Let me think.

314
00:19:00,673 --> 00:19:02,699
- Perfect. Hm?
- It looks good.

315
00:19:02,875 --> 00:19:05,970
- You want to, uh...
- Oh, I'm sorry.

316
00:19:06,145 --> 00:19:08,842
So, what do they say? Only
accountants talk business over dinner?

317
00:19:19,859 --> 00:19:21,828
You kidding me? Huh?

318
00:19:21,994 --> 00:19:23,895
- Did I say something wrong?
- No.

319
00:19:24,063 --> 00:19:25,691
No, not at all.

320
00:19:25,865 --> 00:19:27,493
You don't like going
to parties, huh?

321
00:19:28,434 --> 00:19:30,027
Not really. Ha-ha-ha.

322
00:19:30,202 --> 00:19:32,042
You're telling me that
your idea of a good time

323
00:19:32,204 --> 00:19:33,364
is to argue in court all day,

324
00:19:33,506 --> 00:19:35,546
instead of getting all
dressed up, going to a party?

325
00:19:35,708 --> 00:19:37,006
- Well, I like to work.
- Mm-hm.

326
00:19:37,176 --> 00:19:40,442
- And parties put me to sleep.
- Mm.

327
00:19:40,813 --> 00:19:42,543
You don't like being bored.

328
00:19:42,715 --> 00:19:45,344
It gets me in no
end of trouble. Heh.

329
00:19:46,619 --> 00:19:49,316
Interesting trouble,
I bet. Heh-heh.

330
00:19:56,429 --> 00:19:59,331
- Dinner was delicious.
- Thank you.

331
00:20:00,566 --> 00:20:01,864
We should get back to work.

332
00:20:02,668 --> 00:20:05,536
First day in court
should be fairly routine.

333
00:20:05,705 --> 00:20:07,970
Although the DA
is pretty clever.

334
00:20:08,140 --> 00:20:10,302
He'll probably start off
with a character witness,

335
00:20:10,476 --> 00:20:12,741
- somebody who doesn't like you.
- Okay.

336
00:20:12,912 --> 00:20:15,074
My guess is it'll be
Richard, Gordon's brother.

337
00:20:16,248 --> 00:20:18,513
Could Richard have
had a key to your office?

338
00:20:18,684 --> 00:20:21,586
Sure, but he adored his brother.

339
00:20:21,754 --> 00:20:24,656
If Richard had wanted to kill
anybody, he would have killed me.

340
00:20:30,830 --> 00:20:32,992
Does Stoddard
$ Lyte still exist?

341
00:20:33,165 --> 00:20:36,192
Give some information about
the questioning you'll be doing.

342
00:20:36,368 --> 00:20:38,769
What are you expecting
from the DA's questioning?

343
00:20:38,938 --> 00:20:41,498
Make a statement about
your first day at trial.

344
00:20:41,674 --> 00:20:43,939
The facts in this
case will bear us out.

345
00:20:44,110 --> 00:20:46,204
The people will prove
beyond a reasonable doubt

346
00:20:46,378 --> 00:20:48,904
that on the night of
September 30th of last year,

347
00:20:49,081 --> 00:20:51,050
the defendant left the
banquet in his honor

348
00:20:51,217 --> 00:20:54,016
at the Atlanta Towers Hotel
and returned to his office,

349
00:20:54,186 --> 00:20:57,088
where he savagely
murdered his partner.

350
00:20:57,256 --> 00:20:59,851
The people will also prove
that despite appearances,

351
00:21:00,025 --> 00:21:01,425
at the time of his
partner's death,

352
00:21:01,594 --> 00:21:03,586
Gil Stoddard had a
very serious problem.

353
00:21:03,763 --> 00:21:07,291
The partner on whom he depended
was about to dissolve their partnership.

354
00:21:07,466 --> 00:21:08,627
And with Gordon Lyte gone,

355
00:21:08,801 --> 00:21:11,498
the gravy train would
have ended for Gil Stoddard.

356
00:21:11,670 --> 00:21:13,866
He would have
gotten a small payoff.

357
00:21:14,039 --> 00:21:18,443
More importantly, he would have
been left with no company and no future.

358
00:21:18,878 --> 00:21:20,278
The defense would
like you to think

359
00:21:20,446 --> 00:21:23,746
that Gil Stoddard lost one of the
people he loved most in this life,

360
00:21:23,916 --> 00:21:26,818
when in actuality, he
murdered him in cold blood

361
00:21:26,986 --> 00:21:30,081
to protect the two things in
life he couldn't live without:

362
00:21:30,256 --> 00:21:33,283
Money and power.

363
00:21:35,060 --> 00:21:36,084
Thank you.

364
00:21:36,262 --> 00:21:37,628
By my watch it's 12:30.

365
00:21:37,797 --> 00:21:40,392
This court is adjourned
until 9 a.m. Monday morning.

366
00:21:45,604 --> 00:21:46,902
How are you?

367
00:21:47,072 --> 00:21:48,938
Not great.

368
00:21:51,944 --> 00:21:54,140
I wish I could tell you
the worst was over.

369
00:21:54,313 --> 00:21:55,474
This is some process.

370
00:21:55,648 --> 00:21:57,207
Some guy I never
even met stands up

371
00:21:57,383 --> 00:22:00,114
and accuses me of a crime
too horrible to even think about.

372
00:22:00,286 --> 00:22:02,278
Let's get out of here.

373
00:22:06,859 --> 00:22:08,020
Hey, Gil.

374
00:22:08,194 --> 00:22:09,526
Enjoy your freedom.

375
00:22:09,695 --> 00:22:12,255
I don't think even you'll be able
to charm your way out of this.

376
00:22:13,132 --> 00:22:14,361
- Gil?
- I'm okay.

377
00:22:14,533 --> 00:22:17,059
Let me show you
what keeps me sane.

378
00:22:20,439 --> 00:22:22,271
How did you get
involved in this?

379
00:22:22,441 --> 00:22:24,967
Well, you know, I got
tired of giving a dollar here

380
00:22:25,144 --> 00:22:26,168
and ten minutes there...

381
00:22:26,345 --> 00:22:27,870
- Hi.
- How are you doing?

382
00:22:28,047 --> 00:22:29,767
I just decided that I
wanted to do something

383
00:22:29,915 --> 00:22:31,713
that would make a real
difference, you know?

384
00:22:31,884 --> 00:22:33,944
How long do the
people usually stay here?

385
00:22:34,119 --> 00:22:37,214
As long as it takes them to
get a job, find a place to live.

386
00:22:37,389 --> 00:22:39,688
Our objective here is to
get people back on their feet.

387
00:22:39,859 --> 00:22:41,418
This a wonderful
thing you're doing.

388
00:22:41,594 --> 00:22:43,790
- Hi, Mr. Stoddard.
- Hey, Mike. How are you doing?

389
00:22:43,963 --> 00:22:45,056
Fine.

390
00:22:45,231 --> 00:22:46,961
- Don't go.
- I've gotta go, Mike.

391
00:22:47,132 --> 00:22:49,624
I've got an appointment.
I'll see you tomorrow, huh?

392
00:22:49,802 --> 00:22:52,203
- Bye-bye.
- Bye, Mr. Stoddard.

393
00:22:52,371 --> 00:22:53,669
Bye.

394
00:22:57,309 --> 00:23:00,245
They found Mike
living at the city dump.

395
00:23:00,412 --> 00:23:02,040
I didn't realize you
had an appointment.

396
00:23:02,214 --> 00:23:03,238
So do you.

397
00:23:03,415 --> 00:23:05,043
I made us
reservations for lunch.

398
00:23:05,217 --> 00:23:07,686
Oh, we could've eaten here.

399
00:23:07,853 --> 00:23:09,685
- Here?
- Sure.

400
00:23:09,855 --> 00:23:12,017
You don't want something
a little more uptown?

401
00:23:12,191 --> 00:23:14,717
Watch it. My mother
would resent that remark.

402
00:23:14,894 --> 00:23:15,918
Your mother?

403
00:23:16,095 --> 00:23:17,825
She taught me to eat hotdogs.

404
00:23:17,997 --> 00:23:19,295
Oh, boy.

405
00:23:19,465 --> 00:23:22,663
Any other family secrets
I should know about?

406
00:23:23,068 --> 00:23:24,934
I play a mean sax.

407
00:23:26,672 --> 00:23:28,732
You are a very unique lady.

408
00:23:32,044 --> 00:23:33,842
Thank you.

409
00:23:41,553 --> 00:23:44,284
Yesterday, Lieutenant
Andrews testified

410
00:23:44,456 --> 00:23:47,949
that the victim died of strangulation
and multiple stab wounds.

411
00:23:48,127 --> 00:23:51,063
Based on your autopsy, Dr. Serlin,
would you agree with that?

412
00:23:51,497 --> 00:23:53,466
Yes. There were
multiple stab wounds,

413
00:23:53,632 --> 00:23:55,897
but the actual cause of
death was strangulation.

414
00:23:56,368 --> 00:23:59,099
Marks on the victim's neck
indicates the killer used a wire.

415
00:23:59,538 --> 00:24:01,097
At the time he was strangled,

416
00:24:01,273 --> 00:24:03,640
as you have just stated,
probably with a wire,

417
00:24:03,809 --> 00:24:06,836
was Gordon Lyte sitting up
or lying down in his couch?

418
00:24:07,012 --> 00:24:09,709
Was the killer attacking
him from the front

419
00:24:09,882 --> 00:24:12,374
or surprising him from behind,
and was there a struggle?

420
00:24:12,551 --> 00:24:15,919
Based on the angle of
the wire marks on his neck,

421
00:24:16,088 --> 00:24:19,547
it can be assumed that the
killer attacked from behind.

422
00:24:19,725 --> 00:24:21,318
The bruises on
the victim's body,

423
00:24:21,493 --> 00:24:23,553
in particular, the
bruises on the left wrist,

424
00:24:23,729 --> 00:24:27,723
would indicate the killer was
perched over him from behind

425
00:24:27,900 --> 00:24:32,395
and used his left hand to pin him down
while he strangled him with his right.

426
00:24:32,571 --> 00:24:34,597
Let me make sure
I understand this.

427
00:24:34,773 --> 00:24:37,208
The killer held him
down with his left hand

428
00:24:37,376 --> 00:24:39,174
and he strangled
him with his right?

429
00:24:39,345 --> 00:24:40,904
Yes, ma'am.

430
00:24:41,080 --> 00:24:44,380
Dr. Serlin, would it be
safe to conclude, then,

431
00:24:44,550 --> 00:24:46,644
that the killer was right-handed?
- Hm.

432
00:24:46,819 --> 00:24:49,914
It takes a lot of strength to
strangle a person with one hand.

433
00:24:50,089 --> 00:24:53,526
I'd say most likely the
killer was right-handed.

434
00:24:54,860 --> 00:24:59,025
At this time, Your Honor, I'd
like to introduce defense exhibit A.

435
00:24:59,198 --> 00:25:02,635
It's a video tape of
WNJH Evening News,

436
00:25:02,801 --> 00:25:07,296
which was broadcast
July 18th, 1989.

437
00:25:10,776 --> 00:25:13,541
<i>Gil Stoddard, partner in
the firm of Stoddard & Lyte,</i>

438
00:25:13,712 --> 00:25:15,432
<i>surprised the mayor
and other city officials</i>

439
00:25:15,581 --> 00:25:18,983
<i>at a charity luncheon with a
check in the amount of $500,000</i>

440
00:25:19,151 --> 00:25:20,711
<i>- toward the building of...</i>
- Objection.

441
00:25:20,853 --> 00:25:25,086
Relevance. Mr. Stoddard's
generosity is not the issue here.

442
00:25:26,291 --> 00:25:29,159
If the court will permit, I only
need about ten more seconds

443
00:25:29,328 --> 00:25:30,819
to prove relevance.

444
00:25:30,996 --> 00:25:34,023
- Overruled. LEANNE:
My dear counselor's correct.

445
00:25:34,199 --> 00:25:38,034
This video tape does show
the generosity of my client.

446
00:25:38,203 --> 00:25:43,232
But more importantly, it also shows
that while Dr. Serlin has just stated

447
00:25:43,409 --> 00:25:45,878
that the killer
was right-handed,

448
00:25:46,045 --> 00:25:50,540
the defendant, Gil
Stoddard, is left-handed.

449
00:25:51,083 --> 00:25:54,212
<i>Pledged to match all
funds raised by the private...</i>

450
00:25:54,386 --> 00:25:57,584
The DA will probably
start off with Cindy Forman.

451
00:25:57,756 --> 00:26:00,316
You still think her
testimony will be a problem?

452
00:26:00,492 --> 00:26:04,452
Well, it shouldn't be too hard to raise
reasonable doubt in the jury's mind.

453
00:26:05,864 --> 00:26:07,696
- Oh.
- Oh.

454
00:26:08,600 --> 00:26:10,626
Oh. GIL: No, I got it.

455
00:26:12,671 --> 00:26:14,970
Listen, wouldn't
this be a lot easier

456
00:26:15,140 --> 00:26:17,405
if we just went over all
this back at my house?

457
00:26:17,576 --> 00:26:20,102
I just thought this would
be a nice change of pace.

458
00:26:23,782 --> 00:26:26,217
You know, Gil, um,

459
00:26:27,152 --> 00:26:28,814
we need to get
something straight.

460
00:26:29,855 --> 00:26:32,017
- I did something wrong?
- No.

461
00:26:33,926 --> 00:26:35,952
We can't be involved.

462
00:26:36,128 --> 00:26:37,152
Not now.

463
00:26:38,063 --> 00:26:40,965
This is business,
life-and-death business.

464
00:26:42,501 --> 00:26:43,935
You understand?

465
00:26:45,037 --> 00:26:47,563
I wish I didn't, but I do.

466
00:26:48,307 --> 00:26:50,799
I can live with that, counselor.

467
00:26:54,279 --> 00:26:55,959
Do you have another
witness, Mr. Brightman?

468
00:26:56,115 --> 00:26:59,313
The people call as their last
witness Miss Janet Moore.

469
00:27:00,385 --> 00:27:03,219
Objection, Your Honor. This
witness comes as a total surprise.

470
00:27:03,388 --> 00:27:04,828
Her name is not
on the witness list,

471
00:27:04,990 --> 00:27:06,856
at least not the list
supplied to the defense.

472
00:27:07,593 --> 00:27:10,563
Would both counsel
please approach the bench?

473
00:27:12,097 --> 00:27:14,657
- What is your secretary doing here?
- I have no idea.

474
00:27:14,833 --> 00:27:17,234
Miss McIntyre, we're waiting.

475
00:27:22,641 --> 00:27:25,121
Your Honor, the defense is
totally unprepared for this witness.

476
00:27:25,277 --> 00:27:27,303
At this point, we have no
idea why she's even here.

477
00:27:28,013 --> 00:27:30,778
- Mr. Brightman?
- Miss Moore came to us this morning.

478
00:27:30,949 --> 00:27:32,247
No possible way we could know

479
00:27:32,417 --> 00:27:34,613
she had any information
pertinent to the people's case

480
00:27:34,786 --> 00:27:35,913
until she told us.

481
00:27:36,088 --> 00:27:37,954
Her testimony is
crucial to our case.

482
00:27:38,123 --> 00:27:39,701
- I'll allow.
- But, Your Honor...

483
00:27:39,725 --> 00:27:42,923
Just let the witness
testify, Miss McIntyre,

484
00:27:43,295 --> 00:27:45,594
after which I'll adjourn
court for the day,

485
00:27:45,764 --> 00:27:47,858
and you can have however
much time you need

486
00:27:48,033 --> 00:27:50,593
to prepare for
cross-examination.

487
00:27:50,769 --> 00:27:53,637
You're the defendant's
secretary. Is that correct?

488
00:27:53,805 --> 00:27:55,137
Yes.

489
00:27:55,307 --> 00:27:57,902
Would you say you have a good
working relationship with him?

490
00:27:58,076 --> 00:28:00,875
Mr. Stoddard is the best
employer I've ever had.

491
00:28:01,280 --> 00:28:02,908
Please tell the
court where you were

492
00:28:03,081 --> 00:28:05,107
on the night of
September 30th at 7:15.

493
00:28:05,284 --> 00:28:08,618
I was leaving the offices
of Stoddard $ Lyte.

494
00:28:08,787 --> 00:28:11,086
What exactly did
you see at that time?

495
00:28:11,256 --> 00:28:13,157
Well, I had just pulled
out of the parking lot

496
00:28:13,325 --> 00:28:16,193
when I saw
Mr. Stoddard drive in.

497
00:28:16,361 --> 00:28:18,956
I even honked. I
guess he didn't hear me.

498
00:28:19,131 --> 00:28:22,499
- Are you sure of the time?
- Positive.

499
00:28:22,668 --> 00:28:25,467
I made it home in time to
watch the 8:00 movie on TV.

500
00:28:26,038 --> 00:28:28,398
Wasn't he supposed to be at
a black-tie affair in his honor?

501
00:28:28,540 --> 00:28:29,872
Yes, he was.

502
00:28:30,042 --> 00:28:33,740
That's why I was so
surprised to see him there.

503
00:28:33,912 --> 00:28:37,041
Why didn't you come forward
with this information earlier?

504
00:28:38,250 --> 00:28:41,846
Mr. Stoddard has been
a very good friend to me.

505
00:28:42,054 --> 00:28:44,649
I didn't want to hurt him.

506
00:28:46,024 --> 00:28:48,926
I'm sorry. I just couldn't
go on knowing he'd done it.

507
00:28:49,761 --> 00:28:51,229
She's lying.

508
00:28:51,396 --> 00:28:54,236
Miss Moore, did you do anything
else to protect Mr. Stoddard?

509
00:28:54,967 --> 00:28:56,629
Yes.

510
00:28:57,102 --> 00:28:59,094
I destroyed a document.

511
00:29:00,172 --> 00:29:02,505
And what was that document?

512
00:29:02,941 --> 00:29:07,345
It was a handwritten note
from Mr. Lyte to his lawyer,

513
00:29:07,512 --> 00:29:09,879
instructing him to
dissolve the partnership

514
00:29:10,048 --> 00:29:12,517
of Stoddard $ Lyte, and
to leave Mr. Stoddard

515
00:29:13,051 --> 00:29:15,520
with the smallest
possible amount of money.

516
00:29:16,288 --> 00:29:17,881
Thank you.

517
00:29:22,794 --> 00:29:25,195
What is going on? You said
you didn't go back to the office.

518
00:29:25,364 --> 00:29:26,525
I didn't.

519
00:29:26,698 --> 00:29:28,938
Yet this woman who says
you're the best boss she ever had

520
00:29:29,067 --> 00:29:30,091
says she saw you there.

521
00:29:30,269 --> 00:29:32,204
- Listen to me.
- There is something wrong here.

522
00:29:32,371 --> 00:29:35,034
Why would she testify
that she saw you?

523
00:29:35,207 --> 00:29:36,334
Did you lie to me?

524
00:29:36,975 --> 00:29:38,341
I swear to you, Leanne.

525
00:29:38,510 --> 00:29:40,638
Someone had to have
put Janet up to this.

526
00:29:40,812 --> 00:29:43,304
- What about the letter?
- She didn't have the letter.

527
00:29:43,482 --> 00:29:45,747
She said she destroyed
it. Don't you see?

528
00:29:45,917 --> 00:29:47,317
She's been paid off.

529
00:29:48,086 --> 00:29:51,113
I told you, if you
lied to me I was out.

530
00:29:53,091 --> 00:29:54,923
Don't do this to me, please.

531
00:29:55,093 --> 00:29:57,324
I am not lying. She's lying.

532
00:29:57,496 --> 00:29:59,089
I did not kill him.

533
00:30:00,332 --> 00:30:02,995
- I wanna believe you.
- Leanne.

534
00:30:07,005 --> 00:30:09,133
I gotta clear my head.

535
00:30:09,574 --> 00:30:10,940
Leanne, please.

536
00:30:14,279 --> 00:30:16,077
Please?

537
00:30:23,121 --> 00:30:25,647
Well, Dad. I think I
might have been wrong.

538
00:30:25,824 --> 00:30:27,315
What are you talking about?

539
00:30:27,492 --> 00:30:30,189
And you might have been right.

540
00:30:31,096 --> 00:30:33,588
I don't think Gil
Stoddard can be trusted.

541
00:30:33,765 --> 00:30:36,098
You know, I ask him point-blank

542
00:30:36,268 --> 00:30:38,965
if he went back to the offices
earlier that night of the murder.

543
00:30:39,137 --> 00:30:42,164
His secretary just
testified she saw him there.

544
00:30:43,575 --> 00:30:44,907
Could she be lying?

545
00:30:45,077 --> 00:30:47,569
I told him if he lied to me...

546
00:30:50,148 --> 00:30:51,446
Why are you asking me that?

547
00:30:52,351 --> 00:30:55,515
Well, maybe she was lying.

548
00:30:56,288 --> 00:30:59,554
You know, Dad, sometimes
I really don't understand you.

549
00:30:59,725 --> 00:31:01,455
Why would you say
something like that?

550
00:31:01,626 --> 00:31:05,893
First you tell me you think he did
it, now you say maybe he didn't.

551
00:31:12,571 --> 00:31:15,040
That's Gordon's
brother, Richard.

552
00:31:15,207 --> 00:31:17,403
Gordon Lyte was
strangled with a wire.

553
00:31:17,576 --> 00:31:19,477
Look at that patch
on his arm there.

554
00:31:20,812 --> 00:31:23,043
Special Forces unit.

555
00:31:23,215 --> 00:31:25,275
They're trained to
do a lot of things.

556
00:31:25,617 --> 00:31:28,314
- Violent?
- When necessary.

557
00:31:28,887 --> 00:31:30,378
Maybe you ought to call Conrad

558
00:31:30,555 --> 00:31:35,459
to see what he can find
out about Richard Lyte.

559
00:31:35,727 --> 00:31:37,525
I will.

560
00:31:37,696 --> 00:31:40,097
But first I think
I'd better call Gil.

561
00:31:53,078 --> 00:31:55,673
Miss Moore, you
testified earlier

562
00:31:55,847 --> 00:31:57,713
that you didn't come
forward immediately

563
00:31:57,883 --> 00:32:00,944
because Mr. Stoddard
was such a wonderful friend

564
00:32:01,119 --> 00:32:02,815
and employer to
you. Is that correct?

565
00:32:02,988 --> 00:32:04,047
Yes, that's correct.

566
00:32:04,222 --> 00:32:05,451
How good a friend was he?

567
00:32:05,991 --> 00:32:07,459
I'm not sure I know
what you mean.

568
00:32:07,626 --> 00:32:10,494
Well, was he the kind of friend
that gave you a lift to the airport

569
00:32:10,662 --> 00:32:12,358
or brought you food
when you were sick

570
00:32:12,531 --> 00:32:14,898
or helped you plant your
garden on the weekends?

571
00:32:15,066 --> 00:32:18,127
No. We didn't spend much
time together away from work.

572
00:32:18,303 --> 00:32:19,794
Oh.

573
00:32:20,138 --> 00:32:23,506
Well, now, let me make
sure I understand you.

574
00:32:23,675 --> 00:32:27,339
The reason you protected
someone you believe

575
00:32:27,512 --> 00:32:32,143
was a cold-blooded
murderer was what?

576
00:32:33,752 --> 00:32:35,687
Because he let you
take long lunch hours?

577
00:32:35,854 --> 00:32:37,516
Objection, argumentative.

578
00:32:37,689 --> 00:32:38,713
Sustained.

579
00:32:38,890 --> 00:32:41,860
I saw him outside the building
that night, Miss McIntyre.

580
00:32:42,027 --> 00:32:43,325
Now, that's interesting.

581
00:32:44,029 --> 00:32:45,725
I'm a great believer in physics,

582
00:32:45,897 --> 00:32:50,528
and I don't believe that one person
can be in two places at the same time.

583
00:32:50,702 --> 00:32:54,434
You also testified that you left
the Stoddard $ Lyte parking lot

584
00:32:54,606 --> 00:32:57,576
at 7:15 on the night of
the murder. Is that correct?

585
00:32:58,343 --> 00:33:00,107
I may have been wrong
by a minute or two.

586
00:33:00,278 --> 00:33:04,147
Do you recognize Georgia
license plate number TH4-0I6?

587
00:33:04,316 --> 00:33:05,340
My license plate.

588
00:33:05,517 --> 00:33:08,612
- Do you drive a tan BMW convertible?
- Yes, I do.

589
00:33:09,387 --> 00:33:12,949
I would like to show you a
receipt from a parking garage

590
00:33:13,124 --> 00:33:16,891
at 3231 Rome Street.

591
00:33:18,096 --> 00:33:25,003
It has the make of your car and
the license plate right on the back.

592
00:33:25,170 --> 00:33:28,504
And stamped on the
front is the date and times.

593
00:33:28,673 --> 00:33:32,303
Would you be so kind
and read that to the court?

594
00:33:33,211 --> 00:33:37,307
I'll be happy to help you. It
says here September 30th.

595
00:33:37,482 --> 00:33:43,649
You arrived in this parking lot at
6:53 p.m. and you left it at 7:26.

596
00:33:45,790 --> 00:33:47,782
You lied to us, Miss Moore.

597
00:33:47,959 --> 00:33:50,292
You didn't see Gil
Stoddard that night.

598
00:33:50,462 --> 00:33:52,522
You were nowhere near
the Stoddard $ Lyte building.

599
00:33:52,697 --> 00:33:55,826
As a matter of fact, you were 20
minutes on the other side of town.

600
00:33:56,001 --> 00:33:58,561
And you didn't tear up
any letter either, did you?

601
00:33:58,904 --> 00:34:00,463
Who were you
protecting, Miss Moore?

602
00:34:00,639 --> 00:34:04,974
Whose offices were you
visiting at 3231 Rome Street?

603
00:34:06,144 --> 00:34:10,138
Your Honor, please instruct the
witness to answer my question.

604
00:34:10,315 --> 00:34:11,943
I was visiting Richard Lyte.

605
00:34:12,284 --> 00:34:13,843
Thank you.

606
00:34:14,019 --> 00:34:17,353
Will you tell the court what your
relationship is with Richard Lyte?

607
00:34:17,522 --> 00:34:18,990
I don't see that it matters.

608
00:34:19,157 --> 00:34:21,820
I think it does.
Answer my question.

609
00:34:22,827 --> 00:34:23,954
We're lovers.

610
00:34:24,129 --> 00:34:25,153
For the record,

611
00:34:25,330 --> 00:34:28,767
Richard Lyte is the brother
of the late Gordon Lyte.

612
00:34:28,934 --> 00:34:30,732
How long have you
been seeing Richard Lyte?

613
00:34:30,902 --> 00:34:32,530
Almost two and a half years.

614
00:34:32,704 --> 00:34:34,344
Do you love him? - Yes, I do.

615
00:34:34,506 --> 00:34:37,101
Enough to make sure
he inherited $8 million?

616
00:34:37,475 --> 00:34:39,706
I resent that, Miss McIntyre.

617
00:34:39,878 --> 00:34:41,779
Just as Richard Lyte
resented Gil Stoddard?

618
00:34:41,947 --> 00:34:43,939
- Ha. He didn't.
- Isn't it true

619
00:34:44,115 --> 00:34:46,914
that you and Richard Lyte plotted
the murder of his brother Gordon?

620
00:34:47,085 --> 00:34:49,418
- No!
- Isn't it also true

621
00:34:49,588 --> 00:34:51,113
that you covered
for Richard Lyte

622
00:34:51,289 --> 00:34:53,918
when he used his own key
to enter his brother's offices

623
00:34:54,092 --> 00:34:55,424
and to kill him in cold blood?

624
00:34:55,594 --> 00:34:57,324
- I most certainly did not!
- Then you lied

625
00:34:57,495 --> 00:34:59,726
about Gil Stoddard to
frame him for this murder

626
00:34:59,898 --> 00:35:02,129
because you knew,
with Gil Stoddard in jail,

627
00:35:02,300 --> 00:35:04,667
convicted of killing
his partner, Gordon,

628
00:35:04,836 --> 00:35:07,169
Richard Lyte would
inherit his brother's share

629
00:35:07,339 --> 00:35:09,501
of an $8 million company,

630
00:35:09,674 --> 00:35:13,441
which otherwise would have
gone to a man Richard Lyte loathed?

631
00:35:17,248 --> 00:35:18,546
That man.

632
00:35:18,883 --> 00:35:20,010
No! BRIGHTMAN: Your Honor.

633
00:35:20,986 --> 00:35:22,682
Nothing further, Your Honor.

634
00:35:31,630 --> 00:35:33,963
How about sitting down?
You're making me dizzy.

635
00:35:35,400 --> 00:35:37,528
I can't sit still.

636
00:35:38,370 --> 00:35:41,101
You've waited for
juries to come in before.

637
00:35:41,606 --> 00:35:43,575
This one's different.

638
00:35:45,143 --> 00:35:47,476
That's what I was afraid of.

639
00:35:52,684 --> 00:35:54,414
The verdict's in.

640
00:36:03,728 --> 00:36:05,424
Has the jury reached a verdict?

641
00:36:05,597 --> 00:36:06,860
Yes, we have, Your Honor.

642
00:36:07,032 --> 00:36:08,500
Bailiff.

643
00:36:15,473 --> 00:36:18,443
Will the defendant please rise?

644
00:36:22,347 --> 00:36:25,840
We the jury, in the matter
of the <i>People v. Gil Stoddard,</i>

645
00:36:26,017 --> 00:36:28,350
on a charge of murder
in the first degree,

646
00:36:28,520 --> 00:36:31,615
find the defendant not guilty.

647
00:36:44,903 --> 00:36:46,371
What, lady? No victory kiss?

648
00:36:48,807 --> 00:36:50,002
How do you feel?

649
00:36:50,175 --> 00:36:52,076
- Good!
- Mr. Stoddard?

650
00:36:52,243 --> 00:36:53,643
Oh, absolutely. It's wonderful.

651
00:36:53,778 --> 00:36:56,509
You must be pleased
with the verdict.

652
00:36:56,681 --> 00:36:58,274
How are you gonna celebrate?

653
00:36:58,450 --> 00:36:59,850
I'm taking my
lawyer out to dinner.

654
00:37:13,631 --> 00:37:16,191
I promised you a celebration
when the trial was over.

655
00:37:16,367 --> 00:37:19,599
Going to a tropical island for
dinner is quite a celebration.

656
00:37:19,771 --> 00:37:21,262
You have style, Mr. Stoddard.

657
00:37:21,439 --> 00:37:23,965
Yeah, it's the
company I keep, honey.

658
00:38:39,818 --> 00:38:43,516
- I'm starving.
- Hm? Good.

659
00:38:43,755 --> 00:38:46,623
We've got dinner
reservations for 9.

660
00:38:48,827 --> 00:38:51,126
It's black tie.

661
00:38:51,429 --> 00:38:53,295
I don't have a black tie.

662
00:38:53,464 --> 00:38:55,933
Yeah, you sure about that?
Did you check the bathroom?

663
00:38:56,234 --> 00:38:57,429
What have you done?

664
00:38:58,803 --> 00:39:00,294
Well, this is a
first-class joint.

665
00:39:00,471 --> 00:39:02,271
You know, we have all
kinds of amenities here.

666
00:39:21,893 --> 00:39:23,361
What am I looking for?

667
00:39:23,528 --> 00:39:26,464
On the door, the garment bag.

668
00:39:39,410 --> 00:39:40,742
Well, what do you think?

669
00:39:40,912 --> 00:39:42,972
Oh, I love it! It's fantastic!

670
00:40:26,824 --> 00:40:27,883
Leanne?

671
00:40:29,794 --> 00:40:32,593
Yeah, I'll be right there!

672
00:40:52,850 --> 00:40:54,409
You okay?

673
00:40:55,820 --> 00:40:58,153
I'm so embarrassed.

674
00:40:58,323 --> 00:41:00,849
I don't feel very well.
I've got an upset stomach.

675
00:41:01,025 --> 00:41:03,551
Oh, poor baby. Come here.

676
00:41:08,433 --> 00:41:10,473
You know, it's probably a
combination of everything.

677
00:41:10,601 --> 00:41:15,005
The trial and the plane flight
and drinking on an empty stomach.

678
00:41:15,506 --> 00:41:17,805
What can I do for you?

679
00:41:17,976 --> 00:41:20,070
Oh, there's probably a
pharmacy downstairs.

680
00:41:20,244 --> 00:41:21,576
Maybe they have something.

681
00:41:21,746 --> 00:41:23,738
Say no more.

682
00:41:27,318 --> 00:41:29,753
I know just what to do, sweetie.

683
00:41:29,921 --> 00:41:33,358
You just lie right down
here and relax, okay.

684
00:41:33,524 --> 00:41:35,152
I'll be right back.

685
00:42:01,119 --> 00:42:02,382
Hello?

686
00:42:04,222 --> 00:42:06,748
Hi, Daddy. It's me.

687
00:42:06,924 --> 00:42:10,326
Listen, I don't have much time.
So I just want you to listen to me.

688
00:42:10,795 --> 00:42:12,388
He's guilty, Pop.

689
00:42:12,563 --> 00:42:14,327
What? Guilty?

690
00:42:14,499 --> 00:42:17,958
<i>Yeah, I've got evidence. I
found blood stains on his jacket.</i>

691
00:42:18,336 --> 00:42:21,966
When the police sprayed the office,
they must have sprayed his jacket too.

692
00:42:22,140 --> 00:42:23,768
- Where are you?
<i>- I'm in the hotel.</i>

693
00:42:23,941 --> 00:42:26,911
<i>I sent Gil down to the pharmacy.
He should be back here any minute.</i>

694
00:42:27,078 --> 00:42:28,171
Get out of there!

695
00:42:28,613 --> 00:42:31,447
Not without Gil. I gotta get
him to come home with me, Dad.

696
00:42:31,616 --> 00:42:34,711
Look, he can't be
retried, Leanne.

697
00:42:34,886 --> 00:42:36,684
Like it or not, he's a free man.

698
00:42:37,255 --> 00:42:42,125
I know. I know, but maybe
some legal action can be taken

699
00:42:42,293 --> 00:42:45,695
to keep him from getting Gordon
Lyte's share of the company.

700
00:42:45,863 --> 00:42:48,924
I don't care about the Lyte's
money. Get out of there!

701
00:42:49,100 --> 00:42:52,002
I will, but leave a message for
me. I want you to call the front desk.

702
00:42:52,170 --> 00:42:56,369
Tell them the Lockman case has
been moved up on the court calendar

703
00:42:56,541 --> 00:42:58,601
and that I've got to
get back right away.

704
00:42:59,410 --> 00:43:03,177
<i>We'll take the 10:30 boat
back to the main island</i>

705
00:43:03,347 --> 00:43:07,478
and catch a charter flight home.
I think I can get him to do that.

706
00:43:07,652 --> 00:43:11,054
That way, if he thinks
that I'm working on a case,

707
00:43:11,222 --> 00:43:12,747
I won't have to
see him for days,

708
00:43:12,924 --> 00:43:14,756
and that will give Gordon
Lyte's attorney time

709
00:43:14,926 --> 00:43:17,327
to protect the estate.

710
00:43:17,495 --> 00:43:19,123
Do you understand me, Dad?

711
00:43:19,697 --> 00:43:23,634
Leanne. Please,
please. Just come home.

712
00:43:25,069 --> 00:43:26,367
I will, Daddy.

713
00:43:26,537 --> 00:43:29,029
I love you. I gotta go.

714
00:43:46,090 --> 00:43:47,752
Leanne?

715
00:43:48,025 --> 00:43:51,052
Leanne, uh, there must be a
restroom in the main cabin, okay?

716
00:43:51,229 --> 00:43:53,425
I'll be right
back. I'll see you.

717
00:44:47,185 --> 00:44:48,345
Excuse me. MAN: Yes, ma'am.

718
00:44:48,486 --> 00:44:50,455
I came on board with a
man. Have you seen him?

719
00:44:50,621 --> 00:44:53,750
He's tall. He's wearing
a blue windbreaker.

720
00:44:53,925 --> 00:44:56,417
Oh, yes, ma'am. He made
a phone call and got off.

721
00:44:56,594 --> 00:44:57,892
He what?

722
00:44:58,062 --> 00:45:00,793
He made a phone
call and got off.

723
00:45:10,741 --> 00:45:13,677
- Honey, I'm sorry.
- Oh, Dad.

724
00:45:20,651 --> 00:45:21,983
Hello?

725
00:45:22,153 --> 00:45:24,122
<i>You made good time.</i>

726
00:45:25,523 --> 00:45:27,992
<i>I'm glad you got home okay.</i>

727
00:45:28,526 --> 00:45:32,793
Uh, listen, um, I made a call.

728
00:45:33,331 --> 00:45:35,391
Your case wasn't moved up.

729
00:45:37,201 --> 00:45:38,829
<i>I'm real sorry it
had to end like this.</i>

730
00:45:39,003 --> 00:45:42,997
<i>I know what you're
thinking, but you're wrong.</i>

731
